  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nThe weather pattern is expected to shift late this week with a deep \ntrough getting carved out over the Pacific Northwest and southwest \nto off the coast of California by Thursday. The polar jet associated \nwith the northern stream system is expected to dip farther southward \noff the West Coast, while a stronger subtropical jet across the \ncentral Pacific shifts eastward toward Baja. Although these jet \nbranches are not expected to phase together going into Friday, there \nis likely to be some combined energy reaching the Western U.S. \n\nAs the trough sets up to our west on Wednesday into Thursday, high \nlevel moisture will stream into our region, followed by increasing \nsoutherly moist flow in the lower levels Thursday night into Friday. \nGuidance is in very good agreement southerly low level moist \nadvection will combine with some top-down saturation in the upper \nlevels on Friday across southern and central Arizona. PWATs are \nshown to quickly rise to just above 200% of normal by Friday \nmorning, while low level mixing ratios rise to a respectable 8-9 \ng/kg. This decent moisture is also expected to coincide with modest \nlow level upslope forcing and quite strong mid and upper level \nascent within the left exit region of the subtropical jet. Although \nwe are still four days out, confidence is quite good in shower \nactivity blossoming at some point Thursday night/Friday morning \nacross south-central Arizona and lasting through much of Friday. \nGuidance also shows modest MUCAPE of 250-500 J/kg on Friday which \nshould allow for some isolated to scattered weak thunderstorms. \nShear values look to be on the low side, so at this point we are not \nanticipating any strong storms. Forecast QPF from Thursday night \nthrough Friday night currently averages around 0.50\" across the \nPhoenix area to upwards of 0.75-1.00\" in the higher terrain north of \nPhoenix. The higher end of guidance is at least double that, so it's \nnot out of the question to have to worry about some minor flooding \nissues Friday. WPC has also included this area in a Slight Risk on \ntheir Day 5 ERO.\n\nThe Friday weather event will not be the last period of rain \nexpected over the next week as another likely even stronger system \nis forecast to track east northeastward toward southern California \nand northern Baja on Saturday before moving through our region on \nSunday. There is much more uncertainty with this second weather \nsystem compared to the first as there are notable timing and \nevolution differences. There are also some differences with the \npotential phasing of the polar and subtropical jet streams which if \nit occurs would likely result in a decent low pressure system \ndeveloping within the base of the large scale trough. Models at \nleast agree that there will be as much if not better moisture then \nthe Friday system and stronger mid-level and frontal ascent. Given \nthe uncertainty, QPF amounts are still quite volatile to any big \nchanges in the strength of the system and track. However, so far \nensemble mean QPF amounts indicate overall higher average amounts \ncompared to Friday's event, as well as more widespread coverage. \nRain chances could also extend into Monday, especially across \neastern portions of Arizona.\n\nTemperatures will take a hit later this week and through the weekend \nwith NBM forecast highs dropping into a 75-80 degree range Friday \nand Saturday and even lower starting Sunday. The coolest air so far \nthis fall should move in with the Sunday weather system as highs may \nfall to as the upper 60s to lower 70s for Sunday and Monday.",
